  B.push({ table: { title: "3.8 Script structure and the two implementation hazards", head: ["Topic", "What to remember"], widths: [3.4, 10.6], rows: [
    ["**Overall structure**", "**Initialization · main sections executed multiple times · clean-up**"],
    ["**Timers**", "Must match **a meaningful unit of logical work** — e.g., a business transaction"],
    ["**What is measured**", "**Protocol-level scripts: server and network response time only.** **GUI scripts: end-to-end time.**"],
    ["**Error handling**", "**Default handling is minimal (e.g., HTTP return code)** — **add checks**; **cleanup on error is usually manual**; **verify indirectly, e.g., check the database**"],
    ["**Synchronization points**", "**Wait for an event** to **ensure concurrent invocation or coordinate work between several scripts**"],
    ["**Correlation**", "Replace **recorded internal identifiers that define context** with **variables extracted from request responses**. **Incorrect or missing correlation is the main reason recorded scripts fail to playback.**"],
    ["**Parameterization**", "Replace **fixed or recorded data with values from a list of choices** so each virtual user uses **a proper set of data** — **different enough to avoid caching and concurrency problems**. Some data **must** be unique (e.g., **order name**)."],
    ["**Caching hazard**", "**Results much BETTER than production**, because **data is copied from disk to memory**"],
    ["**Locking hazard**", "**Results much WORSE than production**, because **the software waits for the lock to free**"],
  ] } });

